                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Father's Island is a comedic horror game brought to us by Homegrown Games, the same developer responsible for Into the Dark: Ultimate Trash Edition and Painkiller: Resurrection; don't let the latter fool you due to the franchise name. Before you go any further, be aware that this game is made in GameGuru, and the company's prior two titles are a mangled mess of problems.\n\nYou play as a man named John Richards, who was framed for armed robbery and assault at a convenience store on Christmas Eve of 2009. He was imprisoned for 5 years after refusing to plead guilty to a crime he didn't commit, despite his face appearing on an evidence tape and his DNA being found all over the crime scene. Right before his release, a priest directed him toward an island off the coast of New England, and said that he'd find all his answers there.\n\nTo start things off, let me relay that I love B-Grade horror films; the cheesier the script and the more low budget the better, I say. While Father's Island has that corny B-Grade quality and style to it, its attrocious optimization and performance problems are its biggest hindrance. As of writing, the most recent \"optimization update\" was on September 27, 2016, but the game still runs between 30-45 FPS at best on a GTX 1080 and i7 6700k combo; for non-techies, that's extremely top-o-the-line gear. Also with 16GB of DDR4-3000 RAM and a decent SSD, the title still takes forever to load. These problems could be avoided entirely if the game didn't load its complete, rather large, map all at once in full detail.\n\nOn top of sub-par performance, I could not get a save file to load properly for me at all. Every time I attempted to load one, I'd wait the 2-3 minutes for the ENTIRE GAME to load again, only to be met with a green screen and subsequently a crash. The music within the title is also jarring, as it splices in and out of different tracks depending on where you're positioned. As well, the overall look of the game has a very dark, high contrast level that just makes it harder to see... and not in a pleasant, scary way.\n\nMany of the mechanics can simply be bypassed by climbing hills or skirting around awkwardly placed fences and trees, and generally you'll just be set out to find keys anyway. Weirdly enough it seems that one key fits all in this game, so you'll spend most of your time exploring a rather bland landscape and being taken aback by sudden cutscenes that feature a \"live\" video of a guy laid over a scene from the game. The voice acting is pretty good in a B-Grade kinda way, and it's reminiscent of POSTAL.\n\nI did manage to make it to one ending (out of four) within the half-hour that I spent with Father's Island, but upon loading my save for the third time only to be met with another green screen of doom, I gave up. I thought about revisiting it to try to make it through the story, but quite honestly the performance and technical issues are a complete put-off. No matter how compelling that the story may end up becoming, in good conscience I cannot recommend this game based off of its functionality... or lack thereof. I sincerely doubt we will see it fixed, given the state of Homegrown's prior two titles.\n\n[h1]Rating: [b]1.0/5.0[/b] - It's Bad.[/h1]\nThe Horror Network [url=http://store.steampowered.com/curator/28221963/]Curator[/url] | [url=http://steamcommunity.com/groups/thehorrornetwork]Group[/url] Click for Gore",
